Capsule's change to SDL 3.4.18's Direct3D 12 swapchain. Its buffer count followed the frames in flight,
never below two, and with two each frame draws into the buffer that left the screen on the latest flip.
A display one flip behind what DXGI reports is still scanning that buffer out, and the frame's clear and
draw show there as a band of garbage. The swapchain now holds three buffers whatever the frames in
flight, which costs one buffer of memory and no latency. It needs SDL3-d3d12-frame-latency.patch, which
cycles the fences by the frames in flight and not by the buffer count. Capsule keeps it: sizing the
swapchain by the frames in flight is SDL's design, not a defect it will fix.

diff --git a/src/gpu/d3d12/SDL_gpu_d3d12.c b/src/gpu/d3d12/SDL_gpu_d3d12.c
--- a/src/gpu/d3d12/SDL_gpu_d3d12.c
+++ b/src/gpu/d3d12/SDL_gpu_d3d12.c
@@ -7059,8 +7059,10 @@ static bool D3D12_INTERNAL_CreateSwapchain(
 
     swapchainFormat = SwapchainCompositionToTextureFormat[swapchainComposition];
 
-    // Min swapchain image count is 2
-    windowData->swapchainTextureCount = SDL_clamp(renderer->allowedFramesInFlight, 2, 3);
+    // Three buffers whatever the frames in flight. A frame then draws into a buffer that left the
+    // screen two flips ago. With two it left on the latest flip, and a display one flip behind what
+    // DXGI reports is still scanning it out.
+    windowData->swapchainTextureCount = MAX_FRAMES_IN_FLIGHT;
 
     // Initialize the swapchain buffer descriptor
     swapchainDesc.Width = 0;  // use client window width
